{"reference":"Luke 20","book":"Luke","chapter":20,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Widow and the Temple","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Luke's condemnation of scribes who “devour widows' houses” immediately precedes the widow's temple gift in the next scene, while the following passage turns toward judgment on the temple. Readers differ over whether her gift is chiefly praise, lament, or exposure of an institution that leaves a poor widow giving what she needs to live on. The sequence therefore should not be used to pressure poor people to surrender necessities.","verse_refs":["Luke 20:45-47"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_020_f8250c7248e6fbfdd3ded53d"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}